  "query_preview": "..(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 430 AM EST FRI NOV 27 2026\n\nThe extended period opens Saturday evening under fairly zonal flow\naloft. An approaching trough and upper-level low upstream will back\nupper-level winds from westerly to southwesterly heading into the\novernight hours. At the start of the period, the area is expected to\nbe under fair skies. Whether or not some eastern hollows or\nsheltered valleys decouple after sunset will depend in part on how\nquickly moisture advects into the area. Minor edits were made to the\nNBM output Saturday night to drop some of those sheltered locations\na few degrees to account for the decoupling potential. Temperatures\nSaturday night will cool into the upper 30s to low 40s as the\nincoming system's warm front lifts near the I-64 corridor by 12Z\nSunday.\n\nSunday, the area will see widespread showers returning with breezy to\ngusty winds. As the previously mentioned upper-level low approaches\nfrom the west, multiple models now indicate a 60-knot low-level jet\nat 850 mb, which will help mix gusty conditions down to the surface.\nThe present 12Z run of the LREF Grand Ensemble shows much of the\narea with a 50-70% chance of seeing wind gusts of at least 30 mph\nSunday afternoon and early evening, with the highest probabilities\nof 80-90% remaining over the high terrain of Harlan County. The\nprobability of seeing gusts of at least 30 mph has increased in each\nof the last 6 ensemble runs. With confidence beginning to grow\nregarding gusty winds, edits have been applied from 18Z to 00Z\nSunday afternoon to nudge gusts slightly higher. Winds of this\nstrength bear watching, and wind headlines may be considered in\nfuture forecasts as the Sunday timeframe falls within the range of\nhigher-resolution models. Temperatures Sunday figure to be warm in\nthe upper 50s to low 60s ahead of a cold front, then fall into the\nlower to mid 30s Sunday night in its wake. Gusty west-northwest winds\nwill persist overnight before diminishing on Monday.\n\nHigh pressure builds back across the Ohio Valley behind the front on\nMonday and settles over the central Appalachians by Tuesday, bringing\na stretch of dry weather through much of the new workweek.\nTemperatures will moderate Monday through Thursday, with highs\nwarming from the upper 40s to mid 60s by the end of the period.\n\nMultiple models continue to resolve another upper-level low moving\nonshore near the California coast by Tuesday. By Wednesday, this low\nwill dig into the Four Corners region before ejecting east across\nthe southern Plains on Thursday, though guidance has slowed the\nsystem's ejection speed; this could bring the next round of \nprecipitation to the area later Thursday and onward.",
